The '''First Capuyaquiran War''' (1540–1543) was fought between a Burawi-Guaji alliance and the rebelling tributary peoples of the [[Murani]], [[Dukuri]], and [[Barani]] in the [[Capuyaquiran Sea]]. The war resulted from the tributaries' attempt to gain independence during the weakening of [[Guajicutea]] caused by the [[Great Pestilence]] and the political upheaval of the establishment of the [[Kingdom of Guajicutea]] in 1536. The defeated peoples were incorporated into Guajicutea as overseas territories under Burawi authority, collectively designated the [[Badweyn Islands]].

== Background ==

The Murani, Dukuri, and Barani peoples had been tributaries of Guajicutea since the 1310s–1360s, incorporated through conquest, diplomacy, and trade pressure during the expansion under [[Kanu Muku]] and subsequent rulers. The tributary system required regular payment of tribute — cotton, shells, pearls, wood, food, and marriage partners — enforced by Guaji naval power.

By the late 1530s, the system was collapsing. The [[Great Pestilence]] had reduced Guajicutea's population by approximately 30% and devastated its military capacity. The establishment of the [[Kingdom of Guajicutea]] in 1536 — with its foreign-backed king, abolished religious order, and feudal restructuring — produced political chaos that further weakened the kingdom's ability to enforce tribute. The tributary peoples saw an opportunity to break free.

The [[Boraku]] tributaries ([[Barakú]], [[Weshina]], [[Tulami]], [[Kavura]], and [[Neroba]]) had already been lost in 1538, subjugated by the [[Volisanian]]s as a colonial possession. Their removal from the Guaji sphere demonstrated that the old tributary order was dissolving.

== Course of the war ==

The war began in 1540 when the Murani, Dukuri, and Barani refused continued tribute and took up arms against Guaji authority. The specific trigger is not recorded in surviving sources, but the pattern follows the established tributary refusal cycle: refusal of tribute, killing of collectors, and escalation to open conflict.

The Burawi-Guaji alliance combined Burawi naval and military forces with Guaji soldiers who provided local knowledge of the islands, navigation, and familiarity with the combatants' languages and customs. Guaji soldiers served as translators, guides, and front-line fighters — the same role they performed as colonial enforcers for both Burawa and Volisania during this period.

The war lasted from 1540 to 1543. The rebelling tributaries, though familiar with the waters, lacked the naval capacity to match Burawi ships and the organized Guaji forces that accompanied them.

== Aftermath ==

By 1543, the Murani, Dukuri, and Barani had been defeated. Their islands were incorporated into Guajicutea as overseas territories — under Guajicutea's technical control but subject to Burawi authority. The conquered islands were collectively designated the [[Badweyn Islands]], named for [[Guled Badweyn]], the Burawi explorer who had first contacted Guajicutea in 1520.

The incorporation expanded Guajicutea's territorial reach but bound it more tightly to Burawi colonial power. The overseas territories existed at Burawi sufferance, not by independent Guaji strength. The war also marked the end of the old tributary system: the relationship between Guajicutea and its former tributaries was no longer one of tribute extraction but of colonial administration.

The [[Yakuri]] tributary on the island of [[Yakura]] (tributary since ~1290) did not participate in the revolt and retained its tributary status.

